notes               = "Titled in source (نامه‌ای با امضاء به حسین علیزاده, printed at the head of page 79). One continuous poem across four printed pages — heading on 79, date only at the end of 82 — kept split in raw photos/persian_poems.md to preserve page numbering and recombined here, so page_number is a range. Two readings depart from the literal pass. سنگ‌فرشِ سیاهِ دیده‌ام is an ezafe chain, 'the black paving of my eye', not 'I have seen on the black paving stones'. And جنگل نور is rendered 'the forest of light', though Nur is a forested county in Mazandaran and a place-name reading is plausible in a list of otherwise concrete titles; the footnote records both. The line in guillemets on page 80 is quoted from the 'old poet' named two lines earlier; neither poet nor source is identified. The four compositions on page 82 are invented inside the poem. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben. Photo check 2026-07-31 (p81): برمی‌خواست is printed with the و — nonstandard spelling of برمی‌خاست, kept as printed."
draft               = true

===persian===

آن‌شب باران می‌بارید...
خواب دیدم تو...
در کارِ نواختنِ
نقش‌های رنگینِ یک گلیمِ اصیلی...
و من...
صدایِ سازِ عاشق‌های خراسان را،
نقاشی می‌کنم...
گفتم تو را دوست دارم،
کاش، نقاشی‌های مرا می‌دیدی...
گفتی گوش کن...
و همهٔ نقاشی‌های مرا، ندیده نواختی...
گویی در خواب هذیان می‌گویم... گفتم...
ابر به هوای بوی کاهگل می‌بارد،
و بوی کاهگل نَفَسِ پرنده است...
نَفَسِ پرنده... با سازِ تو... پررنگ‌تر می‌شود....
گفتی:
بوی کاهگل آوازِ پرنده را...
رنگین‌تر می‌کند...
«همیشه آواز برای شنیدن نیست،
گاه خواندن هم چون نمک بر زخم،
برای بیدار ماندن است.»
گفتم: در این شبِ بارانی،
تا آمدنِ تو،
در پنجره، چراغ بیاویزم...
گفتی: چراغ نَه،
آوازَت را، مثلِ چِراغ،
در جانِ پنجره بیاویز،
بی‌ترس از باد... و... نگاهِ بد.
آواز تو خاموش نخواهد شد...
آواز تو، شکسته‌تر از آنست،
که باز هم بشکنند...
آواز باید، نفسی تازه باشد...
در هوایی معاصر.
هیچ پرنده‌ای...
آوازِ دیروز را تکرار نمی‌کند.
تهمتِ تکرار... درعادتِ ماست...
عشق، نگاهِ نو را با خود دارد...
تو، در پیِ عناصرِ اصیلِ تازه باش...
من تشنهٔ نگاه معاصر درهوای قدیمی‌ام...
گفتم: من صداهای معاصر،
در فضاهای قدیمی را نقاشی کرده‌ام...
اما، آبی هوای قدیمی را...
به سربی هوای تازه نمی‌بخشم...
ای کاش آن «پیرِ شاعر» هم...
نقاشی‌های مرا نگاه نمی‌کرد...
می‌دید و می‌شنید...
من، بارها... در بامدادِ سرد.... و...
شامگاه درد....
«از پشتِ شیشه‌ها به خیابان نظر کرده‌ام»
و... واژه‌های سرخِ شعرش را،
بر سنگ‌فرشِ سیاهِ دیده‌ام،
که سراسیمه، می‌دوند...
تو هیچ نگفتی... سکوتِ بی‌تابی،
از چشمانت برمی‌خواست...
دستی، یک جرعهٔ شراب در جامی ریخت...
جام نو بود، شراب کهنه...
گفتی بنوش، مستی، زمان نمی‌شناسد...
تو را دوست دارم،
صدای مرا نقاشی کن...
و سرگرم نواختن شدی.
پنجه‌هایت، مثلِ باران...
همهٔ دیوارهای قدیمی را پاک می‌کرد...
پنجره‌ها باز می‌شدند،
و با هر زخمه... پرنده‌ای...
از دریچهٔ قفسی رها می‌گشت...
با آهنگ‌های تو،
چلچله‌ها سپید می‌شدند،
و... پرستوها، رنگین...
و کودکان فریاد می‌زدند:
«ما از سیاهی بیزاریم...
ما کلاغ‌هایی به رنگِ آبی دوست داریم»
و من...
در مربع خنده‌های زنی که دوست دارم،
آهنگ‌هایی نقاشی می‌کردم،
که عثمان، دوتارزنِ جادویی خواف را...
حیران می‌کرد...
گفتم:
من صدای تو را،
و... دیگران را... که تو را دوست دارند،
نقاشی می‌کنم...
صداهای معاصر در فضاهای قدیمی،
یا... فضاهای معاصر در صداهای قدیمی...
مثلِ آهنگ‌های تو.
درخواب،
نامِ آخرین ساخته‌هایت را پرسیدم
گفتی:
اولین لالایی برای کودکی عثمانِ خوافی...
و... فراغی شاد... و...
زمزمهٔ چوپانی گنجشک‌های جنگل نور...
و... دلتنگی برای...
آهوان و پرندگان یک فرشِ قدیمی...
و... مرثیه‌ای بزرگ برای...
پنجه‌های کوچکِ دخترانِ قالی‌باف...
از خواب بیدار شدم، آفتاب سرزده بود،
اما... باران هنوز می‌بارید...
تهران آذر ۱۳۶۸

===machine===

That night it was raining...
I dreamed you...
at the work of playing
the colored patterns of a true kilim...
and I...
am painting the sound
of the saz of the ashiqs of Khorasan...
I said I love you,
I wish you could have seen my paintings...
You said listen...
and, unseen, you played every one of my paintings...
As though I were raving in a dream... I said...
The cloud rains out of longing for the smell of straw-and-clay,
and the smell of straw-and-clay is the breath of the bird...
The breath of the bird... with your saz... grows deeper in color....
You said:
The smell of straw-and-clay makes the bird's song...
more colorful...
"Song is not always for hearing,
sometimes singing, like salt on a wound,
is for staying awake."
I said: on this rainy night,
until you come,
let me hang a lamp in the window...
You said: not a lamp,
hang your song, like a lamp,
in the soul of the window,
unafraid of the wind... and... the evil eye.
Your song will not be put out...
Your song is more broken
than what they could break again...
A song must be a fresh breath...
in contemporary air.
No bird...
repeats yesterday's song.
The charge of repetition... lies in our habit...
Love carries the new gaze with it...
Be after fresh, authentic elements...
I thirst for a contemporary gaze in my old air...
I said: I have painted contemporary sounds
in old spaces...
but the blue of the old air...
I will not give over to the lead-gray of the new...
I only wish that "old poet" too...
had not looked at my paintings...
had seen, and heard...
I have, many times... in the cold dawn.... and...
the evening of pain....
"from behind the window-panes I have gazed out at the street"
and... the red words of his poem
I have seen on the black paving stones,
running headlong...
You said nothing... a restless silence
was rising out of your eyes...
A hand poured a mouthful of wine into a cup...
The cup was new, the wine old...
You said drink; drunkenness knows nothing of time...
I love you,
paint my voice...
and you fell to playing.
Your fingers, like rain...
were washing every old wall clean...
The windows were opening,
and with each stroke of the plectrum... a bird...
was set loose from the hatch of a cage...
With your melodies
the swallows turned white,
and... the martins, many-colored...
and the children were shouting:
"We loathe the blackness...
we love crows the color of blue"
and I...
in the square of the laughter of a woman I love,
I was painting melodies
that left Osman, the magician dotar-player of Khaf...
bewildered...
I said:
I paint your voice,
and... the others... who love you...
contemporary sounds in old spaces,
or... contemporary spaces in old sounds...
like your melodies.
In the dream
I asked the names of your latest compositions.
You said:
"First lullaby for the childhood of Osman of Khaf"...
and... "A joyful Faraghi"... and...
"A shepherd's murmur of the sparrows of the forest of light"...
and... "A longing for
the deer and the birds of an old carpet"...
and... "A great elegy for
the small fingers of the carpet-weaving girls"...
I woke from the dream; the sun had come up,
but... the rain was still falling...
Tehran, Azar 1368

===lantern===

That night it was raining...
I dreamed you...
at the work of playing
the coloured patterns of a true kilim...
and I...
painting the sound
of the saz of the ashiqs of Khorasan...
I said: I love you.
I wish you had seen my paintings...
You said: listen...
and, without looking, you played every painting I had...
As if I were talking in my sleep... I said...
The cloud rains out of longing for the smell of adobe,
and the smell of adobe is the breath of the bird...
The breath of the bird... under your hand... deepens in colour...
You said:
The smell of adobe makes the bird's song...
richer in colour...
"A song is not always for hearing.
Sometimes singing, like salt in a wound,
is for staying awake."
I said: on this rainy night,
until you come,
let me hang a lamp in the window...

You said: not a lamp —
hang your song, like a lamp,
in the soul of the window,
with no fear of the wind... or... the evil eye.
Your song will not be put out...
Your song is already more broken
than anything they could break again...
A song has to be a fresh breath
in air that is of today.
No bird
repeats yesterday's song.
The charge of repetition... is in our habit...
Love brings the new look with it...
Go after fresh, true elements...
I thirst for a gaze that is of today, in my old air...
I said: I have painted the sounds of today
in old spaces...
but the blue of the old air
I will not hand over to the lead-gray of the new...
I only wish that "old poet" too
had not looked at my paintings —
had seen them, and heard them...
Many times... in the cold daybreak.... and...
the evening of pain....
"from behind the glass I have looked out at the street"

and... the red words of his poem
on the black paving of my eye,
running headlong...
You said nothing... a restless silence
was rising out of your eyes...
A hand poured a mouthful of wine into a cup...
The cup was new, the wine old...
You said: drink. Drunkenness knows nothing of time...
I love you,
paint my voice...
and you fell to playing.
Your fingers, like rain,
were washing every old wall clean...
Windows came open,
and with each stroke of the plectrum... a bird...
went free from the hatch of a cage...
With your melodies
the swallows turned white,
and... the martins, many-coloured...
and children were shouting:
"We cannot stand the blackness...
we want crows the colour of blue"
and I...
in the square of the laughter of a woman I love,

was painting melodies
that left Osman — the magician of the dotar, out of Khaf —
bewildered...
I said:
I paint your voice,
and... other people's... who love you...
the sounds of today in old spaces,
or... the spaces of today in old sounds...
like your melodies.
In the dream
I asked the names of your newest pieces.
You said:
First lullaby for the childhood of Osman of Khaf...
and... A glad Faraghi... and...
A shepherd's humming for the sparrows of the forest of light...
and... A homesickness for
the deer and the birds of an old carpet...
and... A great elegy for
the small fingers of the carpet-weaving girls...
I woke from the dream; the sun was up,
but... the rain was still falling...

Tehran, Azar 1368

===translation===

===footnotes===

Hossein Alizadeh (b. 1951) is a composer and a master of the tar and setar, and
one of the central figures in what became of Persian classical music after the 1970s — which is
what the whole dream-argument between the two men is about. عاشق (âsheq) is not the ghazal's
"lover" here but the bardic minstrel of Khorasan, who sings to his own instrument. عثمان is
Osman, the dotar master of Khaf in eastern Khorasan; فراغی is the maqâm named for the Turkmen
poet Makhtumkuli Fraghi. The four "compositions" the dream-Alizadeh recites are invented inside
the poem. جنگل نور is given as "the forest of light," though Nur is a forested county in
Mazandaran and may be meant.

Two things the Persian does that the English can only point at. شکسته means "broken" and is also
the name of a gusheh in Persian classical music, so "your song is already more broken than
anything they could break again" is also, exactly, "your song is already in Shekaste." And پنجه
is one word for Alizadeh's playing fingers and for the small fingers of the carpet-weaving girls
in the last composition — the poem's turn from the master's hand to the children's happens
inside a single noun.

"The cup was new, the wine old" is the argument in miniature; so is the poet's refusal to trade
the blue of the old air for the lead-gray of the new. The line in quotation marks near the end
of page 80 belongs to the "old poet" of two lines earlier, unidentified.

